                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "First playthrough review time.\n\nAssassin’s Creed Origins is an action adventure stealth game set in Ancient Egypt, and the setting is one of the main things that stands out. The Gold Edition includes all the downloadable content, along with a few extra skins and weapons. The world is large but easy to get around, with different environments, landmarks, and small details to notice while exploring. There is enough variety between areas that the map does not just feel like one big empty space.\n\nThe graphics look good, especially the lighting, shadows, water and overall environment. There is a lot of detail in the way the world is made, and the different areas have their own look. The music fits the setting really well and the sound design adds to the atmosphere. The people, animals and surroundings also add more detail to the world. The photo mode is a nice addition.\n\nThe gameplay is quite different from the earlier games in the series. The combat has a more RPG focused system with different weapons, enemy types and abilities. The keyboard and mouse controls work well, and the combat system is easy to understand once you get used to it. The boss fights are also balanced nicely. There is not a lot of variety in combat, but it does not take away from the overall gameplay.\n\nStealth is still an important part of the game, and exploration takes up a big part of the experience. There are many things to discover while moving around the world. Collecting materials and equipment is simple, and getting upgrades is also straightforward. The puzzles are fairly simple and mostly give optional rewards, so they do not take over the main gameplay. There are some small bugs and glitches as well, mostly involving a few strange movements, but nothing that caused any major problems.\n\nThe Gold Edition also includes both major DLCs, The Hidden Ones and The Curse of the Pharaohs, along with the Discovery Tour: Ancient Egypt educational mode. This mode lets players navigate the open world free of combat and time constraints through guided tours created by historians and Egyptologists.\n\nThe first DLC is short, but it has a strong story and gives more attention to the early foundations of the Brotherhood. The writing is focused and fits the overall theme of the series. It also has a more grounded tone compared to the base game. The new area is much smaller than the main world, but there is still enough to explore, and the design makes good use of the available space.\n\nThe second DLC takes a very different approach compared to the first DLC and the base game. The main area looks excellent, with a strong atmosphere, detailed design and some very unusual locations. The visual side is one of its best parts. The story has some interesting ideas, but it can be a little difficult to follow at times and the way it is presented is not as clear as the base game or the first DLC. The side missions also do not add as much to the overall experience, although there are a few that stand out.\n\nThe third DLC, the Discovery Tour, takes a completely different approach from the base game and the other expansions by turning the open world into a peaceful, museum space. It removes combat, enemies, and time limits, allowing players to focus entirely on exploring the environment and taking guided educational tours created by historians and Egyptologists. While it features no traditional gameplay progression or narrative story, it provides a structured and visually impressive way to learn about the architecture and daily life of Ancient Egypt.\n\nI also completed every side quest in the base game and both DLCs, unlocked all the achievements, and visited every location on the map without missing any. Exploring everything was a nice part of the experience, and it was good to see how much detail and content the game has to offer beyond the main story. The only quests that are repeated are the daily quests, which are randomly generated by one merchant, and the event quests, which rotate weekly. These quests are indefinite, and I also completed a few of them to experience that content. I think this is similar to Skyrim’s Radiant Quests, which can also continue indefinitely.\n\nOverall, Assassin’s Creed Origins is a solid entry in the series. The setting, graphics, music, sound, combat, stealth and exploration all work together well without making the game too complicated. The leveling system is easy to understand, collecting materials is straightforward, and there is plenty to discover across the world. The first DLC stands out more because of its focused story, while the second DLC brings strong visuals and some different ideas but has a less clear story and side content that does not add as much to the overall experience. As a complete package, the Gold Edition provides a long experience with plenty of content.\n\nNote: \n\n1) The [spoiler]Old Habits[/spoiler] achievement was glitched in the past and has since been patched. If you are still having trouble unlocking it despite completing all the required tasks and checking everything many times, you can raise a support ticket with Ubisoft, and they may be able to help with the issue. In most cases, the achievement should unlock normally now that it has been patched.\n\n2) There are very few side quests that require you to progress through the main story before they can continue. So, if there is no marker on the map while you are progressing through a side quest, or if quest objective got stuck and cannot figure out how to continue, try completing a few other side quests and progressing further in the main story. You can then return to the side quest you were stuck on later, and it should become available to continue.",
